{"sha":"3a68bad6248118a1c2d656df5fcf3104bfc964d4","html_url":"https://github.com/thumbor/thumbor/commit/3a68bad6248118a1c2d656df5fcf3104bfc964d4","commit":{"message":"fix: antialias transparent rounded corners using alpha

Transparent rounded corners darkened edge RGB values instead of smoothing their
opacity, leaving a dark fringe, since 168065cbf20a added them in 2019. Apply
antialiasing to each pixel's original alpha, kept in a one byte per pixel
snapshot so corner clipping and both antialiasing passes never make transparent
pixels visible, and keep the source RGB of visible pixels. Pixels the filter
clears to zero alpha get the background color, so encoders without alpha such
as JPEG still show rounded corners. The docs point transparent corners at
formats with alpha. Tests cover PNG, lossless WebP and JPEG output, circular,
elliptical and clamped radii, RGB and RGBA inputs, and spatially varying colors
and alpha.

Encoders without alpha drop the partial alpha of antialiased edge pixels and
show their full source RGB, so transparent corners encoded as JPEG get a hard
edge. With a black background the old code blended that edge into black exactly
as the opaque filter does, so such a JPEG loses its antialiasing, which is why
the documented example now renders as PNG; with any other color the hard edge
replaces a darkened ring. Clipped pixels that were already fully transparent in
the source keep their hidden RGB rather than taking the background color, which
only shows when such an RGBA source is encoded without alpha.

